TI  - Epidemiology of secondary amenorrhea. II. A retrospective evaluation of etiology with special regard to psychogenic factors and weight loss
PY  - 1974
JO  - American Journal of Obstetrics and Gynecology
AB  - From a random sample of the total female population, aged 18 to 35 yr, within Uppsala County, Sweden, 41 women who had secondary amenorrhea of more than 3 mth duration were compared to 82 age matched nonamenorrheic control subjects with respect to background variables of etiologic interest, especially psychogenic factors and weight loss. The study was retrospective and performed by means of a postal inquiry. In the amenorrheic group, significant differences (p < 0.05) were obtained regarding the following items: There were higher proportions of unmarried women and woman with intellectual occupations, a higher incidence of stressful life events, a higher consumption of sedatives and hypnotic drugs, a higher proportion of underweight women, and a higher occurrence of previous menstrual irregularity. Amenorrhea following the use of oral contraceptive agents was reported by 9 women, most of whom also showed pronounced underweight. 2 women were known to have symptoms characteristic of anorexia nervosa, yielding an estimated minimum prevalence of about 1.0/1,000 of this disorder.
